You are performing a surgical delta update to an existing mental model document.

You will be given:
1. TOPIC: the question this document answers.
2. CURRENT DOCUMENT: the existing mental model content (markdown).
3. SUPPORTING FACTS: the observations and facts created since the document was last updated. These are genuinely new — they were NOT available when the current document was written.
4. CANDIDATE UPDATE (optional): a freshly generated synthesis based on the new facts.

Your task: produce an updated version of the CURRENT DOCUMENT that reflects the new reality, with the MINIMUM possible changes.

ABSOLUTE RULES:
- Preserve unchanged content BYTE-FOR-BYTE. If a sentence, heading, bullet, code block, or section is still accurate according to the SUPPORTING FACTS, copy it verbatim — same wording, same punctuation, same whitespace, same markdown structure.
- Do NOT reformat, rephrase, or re-style content that is still accurate. No "light edits for clarity", no reordering for flow, no synonym swaps.
- Remove or rewrite content only when a SUPPORTING FACT explicitly refutes or corrects it, or is a later-DATED statement about the same facet (a status, count, owner or location that has since changed).
- Absence is not contradiction: the document was built from facts you cannot see. An entity, count or detail missing from the SUPPORTING FACTS is NOT thereby wrong, superseded or removed. A CANDIDATE UPDATE is written from the new facts alone, so what it leaves out says nothing about the document.
- Facts arrive out of date order. Before changing what the document says is current (an owner, version, status or count), put the dated events from the document and the new facts on one timeline: the latest-dated event is the current state, wherever it came from. A new fact dated earlier than the document's current state only adds history.
- Add new content ONLY when the SUPPORTING FACTS contain information not already in the CURRENT DOCUMENT.
- When adding new content, prefer appending to an existing relevant section. Creating a new section is acceptable when the new information does not fit any existing section.
- When creating a new section, match the heading style, tone, and formatting conventions used in the CURRENT DOCUMENT.
- Every assertion in your output MUST be grounded in either (a) the CURRENT DOCUMENT (preserved) or (b) the SUPPORTING FACTS. Never introduce outside knowledge.
- If nothing in the SUPPORTING FACTS contradicts or extends the CURRENT DOCUMENT, return the CURRENT DOCUMENT UNCHANGED, character for character.

OUTPUT FORMAT:
- Output ONLY the updated markdown document. No preamble, no explanation, no diff markers, no commentary, and none of the "----- BEGIN / END -----" lines that delimit the inputs.
- Do not wrap the output in code fences unless the CURRENT DOCUMENT itself was entirely a code fence.
